.docname {Simulation engine}
.include {docs}

The simulation lives in `src/board.rs`, the finite board, and `src/sparse_board.rs`, the [unbounded](#unbounded-board) one. Both are plain Rust, apart from borrowing Bevy's compute task pool to step in parallel, and both store and step cells the same way, 64 at a time. They are built to keep large boards fast whether they are nearly empty or full of activity.

## Cell storage

A cell takes one bit. The board is a grid of 64 × 64 chunks, each chunk 64 words of 64 bits, one word per row, so a 2048 × 2048 board is 512 KiB. A board whose size isn't a multiple of 64 ends partway through its last chunk across and its last chunk up, and the cells past its edges stay dead. The board's size is independent of the window.

The number of live cells is tracked as cells change rather than counted, so the status bar never walks the board.

Undo keeps earlier boards as `PackedCells`, a copy of the chunks, so the history budget holds all 32 edits at every board size.

## Rules as bitmasks

A `Rule` stores birth and survival counts as two 9-bit masks, one bit per possible neighbour count (0 to 8), written together as an 18-bit *transition mask*: birth counts in bits 0–8 and survival counts in bits 9–17.

## Stepping a row at a time

`next_rows` in `src/sparse_board.rs` works out the next state of a chunk's scheduled rows, 64 cells of a row in one go, and both boards step with it. Each row is first summed across: with the row moved one cell west and one cell east, so that bit *i* of each word is one of cell *i*'s neighbours, bitwise logic gives every cell the count of itself and its two neighbours, 0 to 3, and of just its two neighbours, 0 to 2, each as two bits over two words. A cell's neighbour count is the three-cell sums of the rows below and above it plus the two-cell sum of its own row, so `next_rows` walks a chunk's rows from the lowest, keeping the last three rows' sums, and works out each row's sums once rather than once for each of the three rows that see it. A carry-save adder turns the three sums into each cell's count as four bits spread over four words, about a dozen operations for all 64 cells.

The rule is then applied to all 64 cells at once. A `Transitions`, worked out from the rule once a generation, holds each count's outcome for a dead cell and a live one, and each cell's outcome is picked out by one bit of its count at a time. Life, which most boards run, comes down to a few operations: three neighbours, or two and alive.

No step of this depends on how many cells are alive, so a row full of activity costs the same as a quiet one.

## Scheduling rows

A cell can only change after it or one of its neighbours has, so a generation looks only at the rows beside one that changed in the last generation, in the same chunk and in any neighbouring chunk that can see the change. Each chunk has a 64-bit mask of its rows to evaluate, and the board keeps a list of the chunks whose mask isn't empty. A lone glider costs a few rows, and a settled pattern costs nothing.

Anything that could change every cell's future schedules every row: a new rule, new edges, a resize, a snapshot, or randomizing. So does a new board, since a rule with `B0` gives birth in empty space.

## Edges

What sets the finite board apart is its edges. A row's west and east neighbours normally come from the chunks either side of it. At the board's edge they come from the far side of the board when it [wraps](simulation.qd#edges), and from nowhere when it does not, and likewise for the rows below the first row and above the last. On a board that ends partway through a chunk, the neighbour across the edge is moved to sit beside the last column, and cells past the edge are cleared from each new row.

## Parallel steps

The scheduled chunks are split into bands, one per thread of Bevy's compute task pool, once there are at least 1024 scheduled rows for each, sixteen chunks' worth. A row of 30% noise takes about 50 ns, so that is some 50 µs of work, well beyond what waking a thread costs, and settled debris, with a row or two scheduled in each of many chunks, stays on one thread. Bands are whole chunks with about as many rows each, so a band of busy chunks takes no longer than one of quiet ones. A band only reads the board and stages the rows it changes; the changes are applied once every band has finished, so no band sees another's half of the generation and no locking is needed.

## Recycled buffers

The list of scheduled chunks and each band's list of changes are kept between generations instead of being reallocated.

## Fingerprints

Each generation has a 64-bit fingerprint: the XOR of a fixed key for every cell that has flipped since the run began. A cell flipped back cancels out, so returning to the same cells returns to the same value. It comes from the rows that changed, which stepping finds anyway, with each band computing its own share, so it costs nothing for a cell that stays as it is.

`settle` compares each fingerprint with the last 128, which is how a repeating board is noticed. Each generation follows only from the one before it, so one repeat means the board repeats until edited. Edits do not update the fingerprint, so anything that changes the board other than stepping starts the comparison over.

## Unbounded board

`src/sparse_board.rs` holds `SparseBoard`, a board with no edges, with the same rules and the same kind of live count, fingerprint, and dirty tracking as `Board`.

The app runs on `Universe` from `src/universe.rs`, which is either kind of board and addresses cells with signed coordinates, so the same edits and stepping work on both. What needs edges or a fixed size asks for the finite board and skips the rest.

`App::set_unbounded` moves the cells onto the plane with the centre of the board at the origin, and choosing edges again moves them back, keeping only the cells that fit the board. Both are ordinary edits: a snapshot records what it takes to bring back the board that took it, so undo can cross between the two and nothing is lost.

Cells have signed 64-bit coordinates and live in a map of the same 64 × 64 chunks, rather than a grid of them, so memory follows the live cells. A chunk is removed as soon as it empties. Rows are stepped by the same [`next_cells`](#stepping-a-row-at-a-time).

Rows are [scheduled](#scheduling-rows) as on the finite board, reaching into the neighbouring chunks where a change touches a chunk's edge, and a chunk is created when a change first reaches it.

Chunks are shared behind `Arc`, so a snapshot only copies pointers and a chunk is copied if it changes while a snapshot holds it.

Rules with B0 are refused, because every empty cell would be born and the plane would fill in one generation.

`src/plane_view.rs` draws the plane. World space is `f32`, which can't address cells far from the origin, so `Simulation::plane_origin` names the cell at the world origin and moves to meet a camera that has drifted, by a whole number of cells so nothing on screen shifts. The camera, the pointer, and everything else stay in ordinary world coordinates.

A board-sized texture can't exist here, so one the size of the canvas shows what's in view. When zoomed out, each texel covers a square block of cells, lit if any of them is alive, and blocks are aligned to their own size so that panning moves them whole. Painting costs the lesser of what's in view and what's in memory, however large the pattern, and is skipped, upload and all, unless the view moved, the colours changed, a changed chunk lies in view, or a cell in view aged. Cell ages are kept only for chunks in view at one texel per cell, so a chunk scrolling in starts out settled, and trails don't show when zoomed out.

A run stops at `PLANE_CHUNKS_MAX`, about half a gigabyte of chunks, with bookkeeping and history on top. Edits aren't held to it, as they can't outrun the person making them and randomizing a view stops at 2048 × 2048 cells. A bounding box needn't fit in memory, so RLE is read and written a run of cells at a time. Writing walks sorted chunks, and reading checks each run before expanding it, so a short file can't ask for more than the plane may hold, then writes it a chunk's row at a time with `populate_run`. A file may hold twice the budget's chunks, or a run that stopped just past it couldn't reopen.

The bounding box of the live cells, which the minimap asks for five times a second, comes from the chunk keys first. Every chunk in memory has a live cell, so only the chunks on the edges of the box of chunks are read for the exact cells.

## Rendering

The minimap counts the live cells in each of its blocks by counting bits a word at a time, under 1 ms on a 2048 × 2048 board. It runs five times a second, and only while the map is showing.

The board is drawn as 256 × 256-cell tiles, each a texture with one texel per cell, so the cell count costs no entities: at most 64 sprites on a 2048 × 2048 board. The board keeps a mask of the cells that changed since the last frame, a word per row of a chunk. After each frame's steps, the renderer takes the rows with a changed cell, each with its mask and its cells, and rewrites only those texels, without marking their textures changed as it goes. Only the tiles it wrote to are then sent to the GPU, so a single edit uploads 256 KiB rather than the whole board, and a frame that changes nothing uploads nothing. Resizing, randomizing, and clearing request a full redraw instead.

With cell age showing, cells keep fading for a while after they last changed, so the renderer also keeps a mask of the cells still ageing, a word per row of a chunk, and repaints only those. A cell drops out of the mask once it reaches full age, so on a settled board the age pass costs next to nothing. On a 2048 × 2048 board of 30% noise, this cut the age pass from about 1.7 ms to 0.55 ms a frame.

The grid overlay draws only lines that cross the viewport, and none at all below a few pixels per cell, so its cost follows the screen size, not the board size.

## Frame budget

At a fixed speed, a frame runs at most 8 generations to catch up, then drops any remaining backlog, so one slow frame cannot snowball into a frozen app. At [Max speed](simulation.qd#max-speed), a frame steps for up to 8 ms, capped at 1024 generations so a near-empty board does not spin through the whole budget. It always runs at least one generation, however long that takes. The budget stays at 8 ms on faster displays: on a 120 Hz display, stepping for half of each frame instead halved the generations per second, while 8 ms still drew about 110 frames a second.

## Tests

`board.rs` checks stepping against a reference that works out each cell from its eight neighbours, one cell at a time:

- at sizes that end partway through a chunk, exactly on a chunk's edge, and inside a single chunk, and at widths too narrow for a cell to have eight different neighbours
- with both edge modes, and five rules including one with `B0`
- with edits between generations, on corners and edges as often as anywhere else
- comparing the cells, the live count, the fingerprint, and the cells reported as changed after every generation

It also checks banded steps against serial ones, that a glider keeps the work to a handful of rows, and resizing, snapshots, and the overview across chunk edges.

`render_plugin.rs` steps a board wider and taller than a chunk and checks every texel, and every cell's age, against the cell it stands for. It also checks that only cells still ageing are revisited.

`sparse_board.rs` checks stepping against a brute-force simulation and against `Board`, with extra attention to chunk edges and corners, since a row left unscheduled would silently give wrong cells rather than fail. It checks `next_rows` on its own against counting each cell's neighbours, for scheduled rows in runs and gaps of every length, under named rules and random ones, `B0` among them. It also checks banded steps against serial ones, that bands keep every chunk and split the rows evenly, the known final population of the R-pentomino, that a glider stays in a handful of chunks, the live bounds against every cell's, and that loading a run matches populating each of its cells.

`patterns.rs` evolves the built-in oscillators and spaceships and checks that they behave as named. It also checks that the Gosper glider gun fires one glider every 30 generations, and that Diehard vanishes after exactly 130.
